    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.36953/ecj.35913212",
    "abstract": "This study assesses decadal climate-land cover interactions in Dehradun (2014\u20132025) using Landsat and CHIRPS/ERA5 data. Utilizing multi-index feature engineering (NDVI, SAVI, NDBI, MNDWI) to enhance class separability by 37%, the research compares three machine learning models: CART, Random Forest (RF), and K-Means. CART achieved the highest overall accuracy (99.88%), significantly outperforming K-Means (89.2%). Findings reveal urban expansion of 33.0% (150.96 to 200.73 km\u00b2), a 6.0% decline in water bodies, and a 1.6% loss in dense forest, with 420 hectares lost specifically in the Lacchiwala Range. Climate analysis shows that the temperature will rise by 1.2\u00b0C, and that warming will speed up to 0.21\u00b0C/year after 2020, which is twice the global average. These shifts have created a 2.3\u00b0C urban-forest thermal gradient and reduced evaporative cooling by 18%. The study advocates for adaptive governance and forest corridor restoration to mitigate hydrological vulnerability in the Himalayan foothills.",
    "field": "Geospatial analysis of environmental change",
    "problem": "This study addresses challenges in remote sensing for complex Himalayan topography, where spectral confusion between land cover types and misclassifications from canopy shadows hinder accurate assessment of urbanization and deforestation impacts.",
    "method": "We propose a machine learning framework using multi-index feature engineering and classification of satellite imagery to assess decadal land-cover change and its interaction with climate variations in the Himalayan foothills.",
    "task": "The task is to assess the decadal impacts of urban expansion and deforestation on land cover and climate in Dehradun using a machine learning approach on remote sensing and climate reanalysis data.",
    "model": [
        "ERA5",
        "CHIRPS"
    ],
